1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,778
On the eleventh of May 2026, 84 malicious
versions of 42 packages were published

2
00:00:09,818 --> 00:00:13,169
through a project's own
trusted-publisher identity.

3
00:00:13,209 --> 00:00:18,288
No registry token was stolen. The project
had read-only workflow permissions.

4
00:00:18,328 --> 00:00:23,872
It published through OIDC. By the standards
of most checklists, it had followed best

5
00:00:23,912 --> 00:00:24,471
practice.

6
00:00:24,811 --> 00:00:29,025
You now know every mechanism that
was involved. A privileged trigger.

7
00:00:29,065 --> 00:00:33,280
A build of fork code. A shared cache.
A release job that restored it.

8
00:00:33,320 --> 00:00:36,425
An identity that any code
in the job could ask for.

9
00:00:36,465 --> 00:00:41,419
In this video you put the links in order,
for this case and seven others, and for

10
00:00:41,459 --> 00:00:45,057
each you name the control that
would have broken the chain.

11
00:00:45,097 --> 00:00:49,681
Hold on to the question on screen.
You'll answer it yourself, link by link.

12
00:00:51,521 --> 00:00:55,384
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:00:55,424 --> 00:01:00,183
This video is a walk through one table:
the case studies of section 21A.18.

14
00:01:00,223 --> 00:01:05,173
Each case is given in the same three
parts: what happened, root cause, lesson.

15
00:01:05,213 --> 00:01:10,420
The cases come from the Phase 0 report and
its sources, and every row has a source

16
00:01:10,460 --> 00:01:13,427
link, which is on screen
while I talk about it.

17
00:01:13,767 --> 00:01:18,768
A reminder of five words from this part.
A workflow is a file that tells GitHub

18
00:01:18,808 --> 00:01:24,001
Actions which jobs to run. A tag is a
movable name for a commit, and pinning means

19
00:01:24,041 --> 00:01:29,234
naming the commit ID instead. A privileged
trigger is an event whose run holds the

20
00:01:29,274 --> 00:01:34,467
repository's token and secrets. A cache is
a stored directory that a later run can

21
00:01:34,507 --> 00:01:39,572
restore. And OIDC gives a job a short-lived
credential in place of a stored key.

22
00:01:39,912 --> 00:01:43,072
Three rules for this video, and
they're the textbook's rules.

23
00:01:43,412 --> 00:01:47,093
No attack detail beyond the
published summaries is reproduced.

24
00:01:47,133 --> 00:01:48,714
This is a defensive course.

25
00:01:49,054 --> 00:01:53,161
Dates and figures are quoted only
as the cited sources give them.

26
00:01:53,201 --> 00:01:58,202
Where the sources disagree, I say so. The
table has a column called "Flags" for

27
00:01:58,242 --> 00:02:01,647
exactly that, and I read the
flag aloud for every row.

28
00:02:01,987 --> 00:02:08,404
And no embellishment. You'll use these
cases to argue for controls in design

29
00:02:08,444 --> 00:02:14,945
reviews. An argument that rests on an
exaggerated number fails the first time

30
00:02:14,985 --> 00:02:16,474
someone checks it.

31
00:02:18,314 --> 00:02:19,892
Here's what you'll walk away with.

32
00:02:20,232 --> 00:02:21,644
After this video you can:

33
00:02:21,984 --> 00:02:26,243
retell each case of the section as what
happened, root cause and lesson, with its

34
00:02:26,283 --> 00:02:26,561
flags;

35
00:02:26,901 --> 00:02:29,645
assign each case to one of
the recurring patterns;

36
00:02:29,985 --> 00:02:33,682
name the control from this part
that would have broken each chain;

37
00:02:34,022 --> 00:02:38,989
trace how a fork pull request led to a
publish without any stored credential being

38
00:02:39,029 --> 00:02:39,417
stolen;

39
00:02:39,757 --> 00:02:43,567
quote dates and figures only as
the cited sources give them.

40
00:02:45,407 --> 00:02:51,892
tj-actions/changed-files, March 2025.
Sources: the CISA alert and the GitHub

41
00:02:51,932 --> 00:02:52,665
advisory.

42
00:02:53,005 --> 00:02:57,839
What happened: version tags were repointed
to a malicious commit that printed

43
00:02:57,879 --> 00:03:02,966
secrets from runner memory into workflow
logs. More than 23,000 repositories used

44
00:03:03,006 --> 00:03:03,662
the action.

45
00:03:04,002 --> 00:03:09,438
Root cause: a stolen bot token. The chain
began with a pull_request_target flaw in

46
00:03:09,478 --> 00:03:13,111
an upstream project, according
to the Unit 42 write-up.

47
00:03:13,451 --> 00:03:17,681
Lesson: tags are mutable. Only
commit-pinned workflows were unaffected.

48
00:03:17,721 --> 00:03:19,786
And public logs are world-readable.

49
00:03:20,126 --> 00:03:23,864
Quick quiz. You retell this
case in a design review.

50
00:03:23,904 --> 00:03:29,531
Which sentence do the sources support?
A, more than 23,000 repositories leaked

51
00:03:29,571 --> 00:03:33,745
secrets. B, more than 23,000
repositories used the action.

52
00:03:33,785 --> 00:03:34,617
Your answer?

53
00:03:39,457 --> 00:03:41,318
B, and the flags explain it.

54
00:03:41,658 --> 00:03:47,638
Flags: there is a conflict about the time
window. CISA gives it as the twelfth of

55
00:03:47,678 --> 00:03:52,543
March at midnight UTC to the
fifteenth of March at noon UTC, 2025.

56
00:03:52,583 --> 00:03:56,928
The advisory says the fourteenth
to the fifteenth of March.

57
00:03:56,968 --> 00:04:02,428
Unit 42 places the mass tag override
on the fourteenth of March at sixteen

58
00:04:02,468 --> 00:04:07,928
fifty-seven UTC. The textbook's
instruction: use CISA's window for audits.

59
00:04:08,268 --> 00:04:13,584
And how many repositories leaked
secrets is not stated by either source.

60
00:04:13,624 --> 00:04:17,974
A figure of "218 repositories"
is secondary and unverified.

61
00:04:18,014 --> 00:04:23,925
So when you tell this case, say "more than
23,000 repositories used the action".

62
00:04:23,965 --> 00:04:26,082
Don't say that number leaked.

63
00:04:26,422 --> 00:04:33,036
Nx, "s1ngularity", August 2025.
Source: the project's post-mortem.

64
00:04:33,376 --> 00:04:37,745
What happened: malicious versions of eight
packages were live for about four hours

65
00:04:37,785 --> 00:04:39,681
and harvested developer credentials.

66
00:04:40,021 --> 00:04:44,486
Root cause, in three parts. A
pull_request_target workflow that echoed an

67
00:04:44,526 --> 00:04:48,559
unsanitised pull request title. A
legacy read-write default token.

68
00:04:48,599 --> 00:04:51,521
And a publish workflow
that could be dispatched.

69
00:04:51,861 --> 00:04:56,122
Lesson: one injectable line plus a write
token reaches a publish credential.

70
00:04:56,462 --> 00:04:57,587
Flags: none.

71
00:04:57,927 --> 00:05:02,963
Map the three causes to three videos.
The echoed title is video 158.

72
00:05:03,003 --> 00:05:08,784
The legacy default token is video 156:
a repository created before the default

73
00:05:08,824 --> 00:05:14,830
changed. And the dispatch is the documented
exception from video 156: events from

74
00:05:14,870 --> 00:05:20,652
the job token start no runs, except
workflow_dispatch and repository_dispatch.

75
00:05:20,992 --> 00:05:25,520
GhostAction, September
2025. Source: GitGuardian.

76
00:05:25,860 --> 00:05:31,427
What happened: compromised maintainer
accounts pushed a workflow that sent secrets

77
00:05:31,467 --> 00:05:35,598
to an attacker's server: 3,325
secrets from 817 repositories.

78
00:05:35,938 --> 00:05:39,020
Root cause: write access
equals secret access.

79
00:05:39,360 --> 00:05:44,571
Lesson: protect workflow files with code-owner
review, and prefer environment-scoped

80
00:05:44,611 --> 00:05:45,634
secrets and OIDC.

81
00:05:45,974 --> 00:05:47,757
Flag: this is a vendor report.

82
00:05:48,097 --> 00:05:51,842
Shai-Hulud and its sequel,
September and November 2025.

83
00:05:51,882 --> 00:05:54,113
Sources: the GitHub Blog and Wiz.

84
00:05:54,453 --> 00:05:59,323
What happened: a self-replicating npm
worm stole tokens, pushed secret-dumping

85
00:05:59,363 --> 00:06:04,170
workflows, and in its second wave
registered infected machines as self-hosted

86
00:06:04,210 --> 00:06:04,673
runners.

87
00:06:05,013 --> 00:06:08,171
Root cause: stolen
developer and CI tokens.

88
00:06:08,511 --> 00:06:13,329
Lesson: a token with workflow write access
is equivalent to every secret it can

89
00:06:13,369 --> 00:06:16,958
reach. Monitor for new workflows,
runners and repositories.

90
00:06:17,298 --> 00:06:22,535
Flag: the size of the second wave is
approximate. The notes cite the same Wiz post

91
00:06:22,575 --> 00:06:26,139
for about 700 malicious versions
and about 800 packages.

92
00:06:26,479 --> 00:06:31,976
PyTorch runners, disclosed January
2024, and Ultralytics, December 2024.

93
00:06:32,016 --> 00:06:35,745
Sources: a researcher
write-up and the PyPI blog.

94
00:06:36,085 --> 00:06:40,813
What happened: persistent self-hosted runners,
machines the project operated itself,

95
00:06:40,853 --> 00:06:44,275
on a public ML repository could
be reached by a pull request.

96
00:06:44,315 --> 00:06:48,816
And, in the second case, a poisoned Actions
cache led to malicious PyPI releases

97
00:06:48,856 --> 00:06:51,200
published through the legitimate workflow.

98
00:06:51,540 --> 00:06:56,171
Root cause: self-hosted runners with weak
approval settings, and an insecure trigger

99
00:06:56,211 --> 00:06:57,117
plus cache trust.

100
00:06:57,457 --> 00:07:02,685
Lesson: ML projects are prime targets.
Use ephemeral runners and treat caches as

101
00:07:02,725 --> 00:07:03,344
untrusted.

102
00:07:03,684 --> 00:07:07,655
Flag: PyTorch is a researcher
disclosure, not an observed attack.

103
00:07:07,695 --> 00:07:10,308
Say "could be reached",
not "was attacked".

104
00:07:10,648 --> 00:07:16,601
Trivy and LiteLLM, February to March
2026. Sources: the advisory, the vendor's

105
00:07:16,641 --> 00:07:18,137
notice, and Datadog.

106
00:07:18,477 --> 00:07:21,807
What happened: a pull_request_target
flaw leaked a token.

107
00:07:21,847 --> 00:07:26,536
Weeks later a malicious scanner release
was published and almost all action tags

108
00:07:26,576 --> 00:07:31,325
were force-pushed to malicious commits. A
downstream LLM gateway library that ran

109
00:07:31,365 --> 00:07:35,818
the scanner unpinned had its publishing
credential stolen, and two malicious

110
00:07:35,858 --> 00:07:38,419
versions were on PyPI
for about three hours.

111
00:07:38,759 --> 00:07:43,634
Root cause: incomplete, non-atomic credential
rotation after the first incident,

112
00:07:43,674 --> 00:07:46,889
meaning the credentials
weren't all replaced at once.

113
00:07:46,929 --> 00:07:48,855
And tag-pinned security tooling.

114
00:07:49,195 --> 00:07:54,685
Lesson: rotate everything at once. Security
tools in CI are high-value targets.

115
00:07:54,725 --> 00:07:55,666
Pin by commit.

116
00:07:56,006 --> 00:08:01,189
Flags: a conflict in the count of tags:
75 of 76 according to Wiz, 76 of 77

117
00:08:01,229 --> 00:08:06,552
according to Datadog. And write-ups name
the account behind the first exploit

118
00:08:06,592 --> 00:08:11,427
differently. Use the advisory and
the vendor notice for exact figures.

119
00:08:11,467 --> 00:08:13,586
That's why I said "almost all".

120
00:08:13,926 --> 00:08:19,072
TanStack, the eleventh of May 2026.
Source: the project's post-mortem.

121
00:08:19,412 --> 00:08:24,352
What happened: 84 malicious versions of
42 packages were published through the

122
00:08:24,392 --> 00:08:28,566
project's own trusted-publisher
identity. No npm token was stolen.

123
00:08:28,906 --> 00:08:33,502
Root cause, as a chain. A pull_request_target
workflow built fork code.

124
00:08:33,542 --> 00:08:37,681
The fork poisoned the shared cache.
The release job restored it.

125
00:08:37,721 --> 00:08:41,141
Malware read the job's OIDC
token from runner memory.

126
00:08:41,481 --> 00:08:45,192
Lesson: read-only permissions
didn't block cache writes.

127
00:08:45,232 --> 00:08:50,751
OIDC isn't safe if untrusted code runs in
the job. And pull_request_target bypassed

128
00:08:50,791 --> 00:08:52,894
the first-time-contributor gate.

129
00:08:53,234 --> 00:08:57,951
Flags: whether the versions carried valid
provenance attestations, and the name

130
00:08:57,991 --> 00:09:02,769
"Mini Shai-Hulud", come from secondary
reports. The post-mortem does not say so.

131
00:09:02,809 --> 00:09:05,479
And no GitHub-authored
post-mortem was found.

132
00:09:05,819 --> 00:09:10,232
Codecov, 2021. Source:
Codecov's security update.

133
00:09:10,572 --> 00:09:15,357
What happened: a modified uploader script
exported CI environment variables for two

134
00:09:15,397 --> 00:09:15,764
months.

135
00:09:16,104 --> 00:09:19,636
Root cause: a mutable script
fetched and executed in CI.

136
00:09:19,976 --> 00:09:23,952
Lesson: remote scripts are the
same risk class as mutable tags.

137
00:09:24,292 --> 00:09:26,073
Flag: history only.

138
00:09:26,413 --> 00:09:29,546
And one sentence of
completeness from the textbook.

139
00:09:29,586 --> 00:09:34,400
A further tag hijack, of the actions-cool
actions in May 2026, is known to the

140
00:09:34,440 --> 00:09:39,191
course only through secondary reporting.
It is mentioned for completeness and

141
00:09:39,231 --> 00:09:40,622
nothing is built on it.

142
00:09:40,962 --> 00:09:42,250
Three recurring patterns.

143
00:09:42,590 --> 00:09:47,420
One: mutable references were repointed
after a maintainer credential was stolen.

144
00:09:47,460 --> 00:09:48,698
tj-actions and Trivy.

145
00:09:49,038 --> 00:09:53,329
Two: a privileged trigger ran
or interpolated outsider input.

146
00:09:53,369 --> 00:09:57,022
The upstream of tj-actions,
Nx, Trivy, and TanStack.

147
00:09:57,362 --> 00:10:02,140
Three: stolen tokens were used to push
workflows. GhostAction and Shai-Hulud.

148
00:10:02,480 --> 00:10:07,913
And one technique recurs across them: reading
the runner process's memory to collect

149
00:10:07,953 --> 00:10:09,477
secrets and OIDC tokens.

150
00:10:09,817 --> 00:10:13,866
The report's conclusion is the sentence
to carry into a design review.

151
00:10:13,906 --> 00:10:17,371
Masked logs are irrelevant once
attacker code runs in a job.

152
00:10:17,411 --> 00:10:22,220
Then the quotation: "the robust controls
are preventing untrusted code from running

153
00:10:22,260 --> 00:10:25,667
in privileged jobs and limiting
which secrets a job holds."

154
00:10:27,507 --> 00:10:32,595
A picture helps. Think of each incident as
a chain, and of each control as a pair of

155
00:10:32,635 --> 00:10:33,083
cutters.

156
00:10:33,423 --> 00:10:38,717
A chain needs every link. The TanStack chain,
as the post-mortem gives it, has four.

157
00:10:38,757 --> 00:10:43,670
A privileged workflow builds fork code.
The fork writes into the shared cache.

158
00:10:43,710 --> 00:10:48,813
The release job restores that cache. Code
in the release job reads the job's OIDC

159
00:10:48,853 --> 00:10:52,179
token. There's the answer
to "No password was stolen.

160
00:10:52,219 --> 00:10:55,735
How?" Four links, and none of
them is a stolen password.

161
00:10:56,075 --> 00:11:01,131
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper.
Write the four links as a chain, top to

162
00:11:01,171 --> 00:11:06,359
bottom. Then take the controls from this
part and write each beside the link it

163
00:11:06,399 --> 00:11:07,948
cuts. Say them out loud.

164
00:11:12,788 --> 00:11:18,592
Not using pull_request_target for a build,
or not running the fork's code under it:

165
00:11:18,632 --> 00:11:24,365
cuts link one. That's video 157, and the
checkout action's refusal since version 7

166
00:11:24,405 --> 00:11:25,985
leans on the same link.

167
00:11:26,325 --> 00:11:30,776
A read-only default-branch cache for
low-trust triggers: cuts link two.

168
00:11:30,816 --> 00:11:34,572
That's the platform change of
the twenty-sixth of June 2026.

169
00:11:34,912 --> 00:11:39,653
cache-mode: none on the job that
holds an identity: cuts link three.

170
00:11:39,693 --> 00:11:41,973
That's video 160 and workflow 12.

171
00:11:42,313 --> 00:11:46,797
Binding the identity to an environment
with protection rules, and letting no

172
00:11:46,837 --> 00:11:50,308
untrusted code or cache into
that job: addresses link four.

173
00:11:50,348 --> 00:11:54,951
Note the word. Once attacker code is
running inside a job that may request the

174
00:11:54,991 --> 00:11:59,951
token, nothing in the job stops the request.
Link four is cut by cutting the earlier

175
00:11:59,991 --> 00:12:00,248
ones.

176
00:12:00,588 --> 00:12:04,083
Where this model breaks: it
suggests one cut is enough.

177
00:12:04,123 --> 00:12:09,354
For one chain, it is. But you don't know
in advance which chain will be tried, and

178
00:12:09,394 --> 00:12:14,110
several of these projects were hit
through a combination nobody had drawn.

179
00:12:14,150 --> 00:12:19,509
That's why the controls are layered, and
why section 21A.21 says to review workflows

180
00:12:19,549 --> 00:12:20,087
as a set.

181
00:12:21,927 --> 00:12:26,544
Three columns, one per recurring pattern:
each case under the pattern or patterns it

182
00:12:26,584 --> 00:12:30,536
shows, and under each column the
control from this part that answers it.

183
00:12:30,876 --> 00:12:35,322
Read the columns. Trivy appears in two of
them, and that's the lesson of that case:

184
00:12:35,362 --> 00:12:39,376
the first incident was pattern two, and
because the rotation afterwards was

185
00:12:39,416 --> 00:12:41,485
incomplete, the second was pattern one.

186
00:12:43,325 --> 00:12:44,596
Step 1: the move.

187
00:12:44,936 --> 00:12:50,466
git tag -f is 🟡 CAUTION: it moves a local
tag. git push --force of a tag is 🔴

188
00:12:50,506 --> 00:12:54,590
DANGEROUS. The five answers,
as section 21A.7 gives them.

189
00:12:54,630 --> 00:13:00,377
What it changes: the remote tag ref. What
it can destroy: it can silently change

190
00:13:00,417 --> 00:13:06,453
what every consumer of the tag runs. How to
preview: git ls-remote --tags origin v1.

191
00:13:06,493 --> 00:13:11,300
How to recover: push the old tag
object back, if you still have it.

192
00:13:11,340 --> 00:13:16,798
When it is appropriate: only for a
deliberate "moving major tag" policy that

193
00:13:16,838 --> 00:13:18,317
consumers know about.

194
00:13:18,657 --> 00:13:23,238
In the first pattern, who is at this
keyboard? Read the root-cause column of the

195
00:13:23,278 --> 00:13:26,473
tj-actions row again before
you answer. Say it out loud.

196
00:13:31,313 --> 00:13:36,572
A forced update of v1, from 71c87ce to
0ca190d. The person at the keyboard holds a

197
00:13:36,612 --> 00:13:39,933
credential that can push to
the action's repository.

198
00:13:39,973 --> 00:13:45,361
In the incident rows it was a stolen bot
token in one case, and credentials that had

199
00:13:45,401 --> 00:13:50,725
not been fully rotated in the other. From
Git's side, a legitimate maintainer and a

200
00:13:50,765 --> 00:13:53,762
thief with the maintainer's
token are the same.

201
00:13:54,102 --> 00:13:56,638
Step 2: what consumers get afterwards.

202
00:13:56,978 --> 00:14:01,905
The same name, a different commit:
5de1e2d. No consumer workflow changed.

203
00:14:01,945 --> 00:14:07,620
And the commit that was reviewed still exists
under its own ID, with exactly its old

204
00:14:07,660 --> 00:14:08,164
content.

205
00:14:08,504 --> 00:14:12,658
Now read the lesson column of the
tj-actions row with this on screen: "only

206
00:14:12,698 --> 00:14:16,404
commit-pinned workflows were
unaffected". You saw why a moment ago.

207
00:14:16,744 --> 00:14:20,544
A workflow that named the tag ran
the new commit on its next run.

208
00:14:20,584 --> 00:14:24,147
A workflow that named the old
commit's ID ran the old commit.

209
00:14:24,487 --> 00:14:29,679
And you can audit your own exposure with
the same reading command, which is 🟢 SAFE:

210
00:14:29,719 --> 00:14:34,785
git ls-remote --tags against an action's
repository shows what each tag points at

211
00:14:34,825 --> 00:14:39,764
today. Whether it pointed there last month
is something only your pins, or your

212
00:14:39,804 --> 00:14:40,962
logs, can tell you.

213
00:14:42,802 --> 00:14:44,450
Five mistakes to watch for.

214
00:14:44,790 --> 00:14:49,626
Quoting a figure that the sources do not
support. Root cause: several rows carry

215
00:14:49,666 --> 00:14:54,380
conflicts or secondary figures; for
tj-actions the number of repositories that

216
00:14:54,420 --> 00:14:57,549
leaked secrets is not stated
by the primary sources.

217
00:14:57,889 --> 00:15:02,877
Telling a researcher disclosure as an
attack. Root cause: the PyTorch case is a

218
00:15:02,917 --> 00:15:06,250
disclosure, not an observed
attack; the flag says so.

219
00:15:06,590 --> 00:15:09,864
Concluding that OIDC or
read-only permissions "failed".

220
00:15:09,904 --> 00:15:14,744
Root cause: each did what it is defined
to do; the chain went through a cache and

221
00:15:14,784 --> 00:15:19,021
through untrusted code inside the
job, which neither control addresses.

222
00:15:19,361 --> 00:15:23,837
Rotating one credential after an
incident. Root cause: in the Trivy case,

223
00:15:23,877 --> 00:15:28,537
incomplete, non-atomic rotation after
the first incident enabled the second.

224
00:15:28,877 --> 00:15:33,663
Arguing for one control per incident. Root
cause: the chains differ, and the edge

225
00:15:33,703 --> 00:15:37,417
case behind most of them is two
workflows that share something.

226
00:15:39,257 --> 00:15:44,305
Now, out of the lab. A staff engineer
proposes three changes to an ML platform's

227
00:15:44,345 --> 00:15:49,521
repositories: require commit pins by policy,
forbid pull_request_target except for

228
00:15:49,561 --> 00:15:54,100
metadata-only jobs, and move publishing
credentials behind environments.

229
00:15:54,140 --> 00:15:59,315
A director asks why this is worth a week
of work, given that "we have never had an

230
00:15:59,355 --> 00:15:59,952
incident".

231
00:16:00,292 --> 00:16:05,058
The engineer answers with three rows, one
per pattern, and for each row the three

232
00:16:05,098 --> 00:16:09,389
parts and the flag. For pins: the
tj-actions row, with the sentence "only

233
00:16:09,429 --> 00:16:13,661
commit-pinned workflows were unaffected",
the figure of more than 23,000

234
00:16:13,701 --> 00:16:18,645
repositories that used the action, and the
statement that the number of repositories

235
00:16:18,685 --> 00:16:21,968
that leaked secrets is not
given by the primary sources.

236
00:16:22,308 --> 00:16:27,544
For the trigger: the Nx row, whose flags
column says "none", with its three causes.

237
00:16:27,584 --> 00:16:32,757
For credentials: the GhostAction row, marked
as a vendor report, with its one-line

238
00:16:32,797 --> 00:16:33,456
root cause.

239
00:16:33,796 --> 00:16:37,012
The director checks one of
the links during the meeting.

240
00:16:37,052 --> 00:16:41,374
The numbers match. The proposal is
approved. That's what the flags are for.

