257
00:17:54,678 --> 00:17:56,338
You should now be able to say:

258
00:17:56,678 --> 00:18:01,166
Each case has three parts, what happened,
root cause and lesson, and a flag that

259
00:18:01,206 --> 00:18:03,430
says how far its figures can be trusted.

260
00:18:03,770 --> 00:18:08,594
Three patterns recur: mutable references
repointed after a credential theft; a

261
00:18:08,634 --> 00:18:13,645
privileged trigger that ran or interpolated
outsider input; stolen tokens used to

262
00:18:13,685 --> 00:18:14,581
push workflows.

263
00:18:14,921 --> 00:18:19,707
One technique recurs across them: reading
runner memory, which makes masked logs

264
00:18:19,747 --> 00:18:22,361
irrelevant once attacker
code runs in a job.

265
00:18:22,701 --> 00:18:27,762
In the TanStack chain no stored credential
was stolen: fork code under a privileged

266
00:18:27,802 --> 00:18:32,862
trigger, a poisoned shared cache, a release
job that restored it, and an OIDC token

267
00:18:32,902 --> 00:18:33,907
read from memory.

268
00:18:34,247 --> 00:18:38,988
The robust controls are preventing untrusted
code from running in privileged jobs

269
00:18:39,028 --> 00:18:41,289
and limiting which secrets a job holds.

